# CI Pipeline

## Structure

- `pr-ci.yml` — triggers on PRs targeting `main` or `master`, runs lint → type check → test
- `publish.yml` — triggers on push to `master` and on `vX.Y.Z` tags; builds the `web`
  and `worker` images and pushes versioned tags to GHCR
  (`ghcr.io/etorhub/dossier-web`, `ghcr.io/etorhub/dossier-worker`). The NAS **pulls**
  these prebuilt images (selected by `DOSSIER_TAG`); it does not build from source.
  Portainer redeploys via polling + re-pull. See `docs/DEPLOYMENT_PORTAINER.md`.

This is still a single-deployment-target project (NAS only) — `publish.yml` exists to
move builds off the CPU-bound NAS and to give versioned, roll-back-able images, not to
add a second target. Don't add deploy workflows for other targets (Pi, GPU, cloud).

Both `main` and `master` are **protected branches**. Branch protection requires the `ci` check from `pr-ci.yml` to pass before a PR can be merged.

## CI job (pr-ci.yml)

```yaml
- name: Lint (ruff)
  run: ruff check .

- name: Type check (mypy)
  run: mypy app/

- name: Test (pytest)
  run: pytest
```

All three steps must pass. Do not remove or skip any of them.

## What not to do

- Do **not** bypass branch protection or merge without a passing `ci` check.
- Do **not** add deploy workflows for other targets (Pi/GPU/cloud) — there is one
  deployment target (the NAS). `publish.yml` builds images **for that one target**;
  keep it that way.
- Do **not** put `build:` directives back into `docker-compose.yml` — production pulls
  `image:` from GHCR. Local build context lives in `docker-compose.override.yml`.
